    "title": "How to Watch St. John's vs Duke: Time, CBS Broadcast, & Postseason Matchup Details",
    "text": "© 2026 SaturdayDownSouth.com Home > News > Sports > How to Watch St. John’s vs Duke: Time, CBS Broadcast, & Postseason Matchup Details Ad Disclosure This article/post contains references to products or services from one or more of our advertisers or partners. We may receive compensation when you click on links to those products or services. The NCAA Men’s Basketball postseason brings a compelling top-10 matchup to Washington, DC. The No. 10 St. John’s Red Storm take on the No. 1 Duke Blue Devils in a highly anticipated contest. Both teams enter this neutral-site game leading their respective conference standings. Duke paces the ACC and St. John’s sits atop the Big East, with both programs holding 0-0 conference marks. Watch St. John’s vs Duke on Paramount+. Head coach Jon Scheyer guides his top-ranked Blue Devils, who hold a 32-2 overall record. They face Rick Pitino’s 28-6 Red Storm squad. St. John’s wants to topple the top team to advance their postseason run. Meanwhile, Duke looks to validate their number one ranking and add to their five national championships. Read on to find the official tip-off time, venue location, CBS television channel details, and streaming options. When is St. John’s vs Duke? What Time Does it Start? The postseason matchup between the St. John’s Red Storm and the Duke Blue Devils is scheduled for March 27, 2026. Tip-off is set for 7:10 PM ET. This contest will be played at Capital One Arena in Washington, DC. Where is St. John’s vs Duke? This NCAA Men’s Basketball game takes place at Capital One Arena in Washington, DC. Because this is a neutral-site tournament game, the Duke Blue Devils are officially designated as the home team. The St. John’s Red Storm will serve as the away team for this matchup. How Can I Watch St. John’s vs Duke? The game between the St. John’s Red Storm and the Duke Blue Devils will be broadcast on television via CBS. Fans looking to follow the action live can tune into the CBS television network. CBS holds the exclusive television rights for this contest, with the broadcast beginning at 7:10 PM ET. How Can I Stream St. John’s vs Duke? This matchup is broadcast on the CBS network. Subscribers can utilize the Paramount+ platform to access their live local CBS station and stream the game as it begins at 7:10 PM ET. Fans can also stream the game live on Fubo, which carries CBS and offers access to your local CBS affiliate. Fubo is an excellent cord-cutting option for those who want to watch the game without a traditional cable subscription. While other NCAA games on Turner Sports networks often stream on HBO Max, this specific CBS broadcast requires a platform providing access to your local CBS station, making Fubo and Paramount+ the top streaming choices. Matchup Preview and Market Outlook Before diving into the broadcast details, let us look at the prediction markets. Fans who want to put their basketball knowledge to the test can find value on Kalshi. Kalshi allows you to trade on the actual outcome of the game. You can check the platform to see how traders are pricing the likelihood of a Duke victory compared to a St. John’s upset. This turns your reads on the game into a tradable opportunity. Who Will Win? Data from Kalshi on March 25th. Prediction markets offer a clear look at how traders view this matchup. The Duke Blue Devils are the clear favorite to win this postseason game. Current prediction market data gives Duke an implied win probability of 71.0%. Conversely, the St. John’s Red Storm enter the game with a 29.0% win probability. Given the 42.0% gap between these implied probabilities, the market suggests Duke is highly likely to secure the victory over St. John’s. Traders are heavily backing the top-ranked Blue Devils to advance, though the Red Storm still hold enough probability to keep the market active. Creative Manager and Writer at Sportradar. Craig has previously worked as a camera operator and video director in radio and television, as well as 